\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations (14B)}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

At 14B (Qwen2.5-14B), self-reference yields 50.8\% ($2{\times}$ chance, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$); switching to cross-reference (Pythia-1.4B) shifts accuracy by $-$6.8\,pp to 44.1\% but raises $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}$ to 2 for nucleus and temperature; reference-model choice is therefore a major confound in cross-scale comparisons (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
At matched 1.5B scale, instruction tuning preserves depth signal (Appendix~\ref{app:instruct_1p5b}).
Cross-domain transfer to arXiv abstracts reaches 74.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:domain_bias}).

\paragraph{Partial-mixing (accumulation).}
\label{sec:gen:accumulation}
Real-world contamination mixes synthetic and human text rather than fully replacing the training set.
We test robustness under 25\% and 50\% synthetic mixing across three model--strategy cells (Table~\ref{tab:accum_auc}).
At 25\% mixing, all three cells preserve $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}3$ with 8--15\,pp accuracy attenuation relative to full replacement.
At 50\% mixing, 2/3 cells retain $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$; the sole failure (GPT-2~XL, d2v3 AUC\,=\,0.695) falls 0.5\,pp below threshold.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our benchmark uses full-replacement chains at 1--1.5B parameters; real-world contamination involves accumulation at unknown mixing ratios (per-passage robustness in Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_depth}), and depth signal attenuates at larger scales (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale}), with reference-model mismatch contributing to the observed degradation (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
Cross-strategy transfer can invert adjacent-depth predictions, requiring strategy-aware deployment (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}).
On held-out web-crawl corpora, pooled-classifier raw false-positive rates range from 15\% to 34\%, reducible to ${\leq}$7\% via posterior-threshold calibration (Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}); robustness to post-hoc paraphrasing remains untested.

\section{LoRA Fine-Tuning Protocol}
\label{app:lora_protocol}

Table~\ref{tab:lora_protocol} lists the full LoRA fine-tuning configuration used for recursive chain construction (\S\ref{sec:method:benchmark}).
At each depth $k$, a fresh LoRA adapter is initialized on the base model and trained on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$; adapters are not inherited across depths.
The HuggingFace Trainer default linear decay schedule is used with no warmup.
Target modules are model-specific: \texttt{query\_key\_value} (Pythia), \texttt{att\_proj} (OLMo), and \texttt{c\_attn} (GPT-2~XL).
Cross-scale 7--8B experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}) use QLoRA (4-bit NF4) with $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$; the 14B model uses FP16 LoRA with the same rank and scaling.

